    "answer": {
      "short": "Three distinct roots would force $a=b=c=0$, contradicting $a\\ne0$. Hence there are at most two distinct roots.\n\nSubtract the substituted root equations and factor their differences.",
      "worked": "Suppose there are three distinct roots $\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}a\\alpha^2+b\\alpha+c&=0\\\\a\\beta^2+b\\beta+c&=0\\\\a\\gamma^2+b\\gamma+c&=0\\end{align*}$$\nSubtracting the second equation from the first gives\n$$\\begin{align*}a(\\alpha^2-\\beta^2)+b(\\alpha-\\beta)&=0\\\\(\\alpha-\\beta)\\bigl(a(\\alpha+\\beta)+b\\bigr)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\alpha\\ne\\beta$,\n$$a(\\alpha+\\beta)+b=0.$$\nSimilarly, subtracting the third equation from the first and using $\\alpha\\ne\\gamma$ gives\n$$a(\\alpha+\\gamma)+b=0.$$\nSubtracting these last two equations gives\n$$\\begin{align*}a(\\beta-\\gamma)&=0\\\\a&=0,\\end{align*}$$\nbecause $\\beta\\ne\\gamma$. Substitution into $a(\\alpha+\\beta)+b=0$ gives $b=0$, and then $a\\alpha^2+b\\alpha+c=0$ gives $c=0$.\nThus $a=b=c=0$, contradicting $a\\ne0$. Therefore a quadratic equation cannot have more than two distinct roots.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
